===Etymology===
{{m|en|bumper}} + {{m|en|sticker}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|US}} An [[adhesive]] [[label]] or [[decal]], usually applied to the bumper or back end of an automobile, and displaying a short political, promotional, philosophical, or humorous message.
#: {{syn|en|car-sticker|q1=UK}}
#: {{ux|en|He has a '''bumper sticker''' on his van that reads "faster than a speeding ticket".}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Eric Schlosser|title=Fast Food Nation|year=2012|origyear=2001|publisher=Houghton Mifflin Harcourt|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=dU13X_AM_N8C&pg=PA65&dq=%22Californicate%22&hl=&cd=18&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22Californicate%22&f=false|isbn=978-0-547-75033-0|page=65|passage=Many longtime residents strongly oppose the extremism of the newcomers, sporting '''bumper stickers''' that say, “Don't Californicate Colorado.”}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Pagan Kennedy|title=Who Made That Bumper Sticker?|work=The New York Times|date=2012-09-21|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/magazine/who-made-that-bumper-sticker.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Are '''bumper stickers''', as a folk art, on the decline? Yes — in fact, a lot of luxury cars don’t even have bumpers anymore.}}
# {{lb|en|US|figurative}} Said of [[can]]ned insight; a [[slogan]] or [[saying]] that is [[trite]] or lacks [[depth]].
#: {{co|en|'''bumper-sticker''' philosophy}}

